<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Gibbon Wakefield</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a>–<a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Gibbon Wakefield was a British colonial theorist who argued that colonial land should be sold at a sufficient price to fund the emigration of free labourers. Developed while he was in prison for abducting an heiress, his ideas shaped the founding of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1836 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Company's settlements. He later moved to New Zealand, where he sat in its first parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon-wakefield.jpg" alt="Edward Gibbon Wakefield" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Holl (1808-1884) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Gibbon_Wakefield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
